  "query_preview": "..Clearing skies behind today's cold front will make\nfor quite the crisp night across Utah and southwest Wyoming, with\nsome of the coldest low temperatures of the season so far. Many \nlower elevation valleys will see temperatures dip into the low to \nmid 30s, except the low 40s across lower Washington County. Given \nrecent rainfall and clearing skies tonight, there is a 20% chance \nfor fog-prone areas across the Cache Valley and Wasatch Back to \nsee freezing fog develop overnight. The limiting factor will be \nhow quickly a deck of high clouds moves into the region, as these \nclouds could limit radiational cooling overnight. \n\nHigh pressure builds in across the Intermountain West on Sunday\ninto Monday, keeping conditions dry and mild through this period.\nThis pattern will be brief, as another system is slated to impact\nthe region on Tuesday. This next system looks quite similar that\nof today's: a fast-moving negatively tilted trough swinging\nthrough the Great Basin, bringing widespread rain and mountain\nsnow ahead of a cold front. As such, likely looking at similar \nimpacts with this upcoming storm. H7 temperatures around -4 to -5C\nindicate snow levels as low as 7000 feet, though best chances for\naccumulating snowfall will generally be above 8000-9000 feet.\nCurrent forecast calls for snowfall amounts between 3 to 8 inches\nfor most mountains above 8000 feet, except locally up to 10 inches\nfor the southern mountains. \n\nBehind the cold front passage late Tuesday, precipitation\ngradually tapers off through early Wednesday, drying out briefly\nahead of yet another storm expected to impact our area on Thursday\ninto Friday. There is more considerable uncertainty regarding this\nlate-week storm, as models struggle to converge on the track and\nmagnitude of the associated closed low. While this system is\nlikely to bring another round of widespread precipitation, the\nuncertainty lies in the precipitation accumulations and just how\ncold of an airmass this storm brings to the region. Current H7\ntemperature spread late Thursday is between -3 to -9C, with the\nlatter supportive of snow down to valley levels. This scenario is\nstill on the lower end, with around 20% of models resolving this\ncolder airmass, but still something to keep an eye on as we get\ncloser.",
